  ('ab53a43e-ade8-5553-bbab-3ea6333d0861', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'graphing-linear-functions'), null, 'easy',
   $q$To graph $y = \frac{2}{3}x + 1$, Dariela plots $(0, 1)$ first. Which move from that point lands on another point of the line?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Right 3, up 2","is_correct":true},{"text":"Right 2, up 3","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"inverted-rise-and-run"},{"text":"Right 3, down 2","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-sign-of-slope"},{"text":"Right 3, up 1","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-intercept-as-rise"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Right 3, up 2$q$, $q$Slope is rise over run, so $\frac{2}{3}$ means up 2 for every 3 to the right. From $(0, 1)$, moving right 3 and up 2 reaches $(3, 3)$, and indeed $\frac{2}{3}(3) + 1 = 3$. Moving right 2, up 3 uses the slope $\frac{3}{2}$ instead.$q$, 'draft', null)
