   $q$At a bowling alley, the total cost is a linear function of the number of games. Two games cost 13 dollars and five games cost 26.5 dollars. What is the $y$-intercept of the graph, and what does it mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"13 dollars, the shoe rental fee paid before any games","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-first-point-as-intercept"},{"text":"4.5 dollars, the shoe rental fee paid before any games","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-slope-with-intercept"},{"text":"4 dollars, the shoe rental fee paid before any games","is_correct":true},{"text":"4 dollars, the cost of each game","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misinterpreted-intercept-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$4 dollars, the shoe rental fee paid before any games$q$, $q$The rate is $\frac{26.5 - 13}{5 - 2} = \frac{13.5}{3} = 4.5$ dollars per game. Then $13 = 4.5(2) + b$, so $b = 13 - 9 = 4$. The $y$-intercept is the cost for $0$ games, a fixed 4 dollar fee. The value 4.5 dollars is the slope, the cost of each game.$q$, 'draft', null)
