  ('cd1a180c-e499-5ba9-8338-401b19e19242', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'geometry-proofs'), null, 'medium',
   $qq$A proof shows that alternate interior angles are congruent. Given: lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, cut by transversal $t$. $\angle 1$ and $\angle 5$ are corresponding angles, $\angle 5$ and $\angle 4$ are vertical angles, and $\angle 1$ and $\angle 4$ are alternate interior angles. Step 1: $\angle 1 \cong \angle 5$. Step 2: $\angle 5 \cong \angle 4$ (Vertical Angles Theorem). Step 3: $\angle 1 \cong \angle 4$ (Transitive Property). Which reason justifies Step 1?$qq$, $q$[{"text":"Corresponding Angles Postulate","is_correct":true},{"text":"Converse of the Corresponding Angles Postulate","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-the-converse-instead-of-the-postulate"},{"text":"Vertical Angles Theorem","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"called-corresponding-angles-vertical"},{"text":"Linear Pair Postulate","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"called-corresponding-angles-a-linear-pair"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Corresponding Angles Postulate$q$, $q$The lines are given as parallel and the conclusion is that two corresponding angles are congruent, which is the Corresponding Angles Postulate. Its converse goes the other way: it starts with congruent corresponding angles and concludes the lines are parallel.$q$, 'draft', null)
