  ('fc9f1ada-eed1-5593-834a-f47e1d83aedd', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'geometry-proofs'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Lines $a$ and $b$ are cut by transversal $t$. Alternate interior angles $\angle 3$ and $\angle 6$ are both $64^\circ$. Nothing else is given. Which conclusion and reason are correct?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$a \\parallel b$, by the Alternate Interior Angles Theorem","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-the-theorem-instead-of-its-converse"},{"text":"$\\angle 3$ and $\\angle 6$ are supplementary, by the Linear Pair Postulate","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"called-alternate-interior-a-linear-pair"},{"text":"$a \\parallel b$, by the Converse of the Alternate Interior Angles Theorem","is_correct":true},{"text":"$a \\perp t$, because the angles are congruent","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-congruent-with-perpendicular"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$a \parallel b$, by the Converse of the Alternate Interior Angles Theorem$q$, $q$Here the congruent angles are known and parallel lines are the conclusion, so the converse is the reason. The Alternate Interior Angles Theorem itself assumes the lines are already parallel, so it cannot be used to prove that they are.$q$, 'draft', null)
