  ('8755161d-bec0-5cf9-8f58-df83d7c2f942', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'geometry-proofs'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Two different points $C$ and $D$ are each equidistant from points $A$ and $B$, so $CA = CB$ and $DA = DB$. Which conclusion is justified?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Line $CD$ is parallel to $AB$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-perpendicular-with-parallel"},{"text":"$C$ is the midpoint of $AB$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-equidistant-point-must-be-midpoint"},{"text":"Line $CD$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$CD = AB$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-equal-segment-lengths"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Line $CD$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB$$q$, $q$By the converse of the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem, both $C$ and $D$ lie on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$. Two different points determine a line, so line $CD$ is that perpendicular bisector. Neither point has to be the midpoint, and the length of $CD$ is not determined.$q$, 'draft', null)
