   $q$Given: line $\ell$ is perpendicular to $AB$ at its midpoint $M$, and $P$ is on $\ell$. Prove: $PA = PB$. Step 1: $AM = MB$. Step 2: $\angle PMA \cong \angle PMB$. Step 3: $PM = PM$. Step 4: by SAS. Step 5: $PA = PB$ (corresponding parts of congruent triangles). Which statement completes Step 4?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\triangle PMA \\cong \\triangle BMP$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"mismatched-corresponding-vertices"},{"text":"$\\angle PAM \\cong \\angle PBM$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-a-consequence-as-the-sas-step"},{"text":"$PA = PB$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"stated-the-conclusion-early"},{"text":"$\\triangle PMA \\cong \\triangle PMB$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\triangle PMA \cong \triangle PMB$$q$, $q$SAS proves two triangles congruent, matching $P$ to $P$, $M$ to $M$ and $A$ to $B$, so Step 4 is $\triangle PMA \cong \triangle PMB$. Writing $\triangle BMP$ pairs $P$ with $B$, which does not match the sides used. The angle statement and $PA = PB$ both come after the triangles are known to be congruent.$q$, 'draft', null)
