   $q$Given: $\angle 1$ and $\angle 2$ are supplementary. $\angle 2$ and $\angle 3$ form a linear pair. For lines $a$ and $b$ cut by transversal $t$, $\angle 1$ and $\angle 3$ are corresponding angles. Prove: $a \parallel b$. Step 1: $\angle 1$ and $\angle 2$ are supplementary (Given). Step 2: $\angle 2$ and $\angle 3$ are supplementary (Linear Pair Postulate). Step 3: $\angle 1 \cong \angle 3$ (Congruent Supplements Theorem). Step 4: $a \parallel b$. Which reason justifies Step 4?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Corresponding Angles Postulate","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-the-postulate-instead-of-its-converse"},{"text":"Converse of the Alternate Interior Angles Theorem","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misidentified-the-angle-pair"},{"text":"Converse of the Corresponding Angles Postulate","is_correct":true},{"text":"Vertical Angles Theorem","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"called-corresponding-angles-vertical"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Converse of the Corresponding Angles Postulate$q$, $q$Step 3 shows a pair of corresponding angles are congruent, and the converse of the Corresponding Angles Postulate turns that into $a \parallel b$. The postulate itself assumes parallel lines, and the alternate interior converse does not apply because $\angle 1$ and $\angle 3$ are corresponding, not alternate interior.$q$, 'draft', null)
