  ('cc720a0c-4d7b-546a-8bf3-386c8c581e08', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'geometry-proofs'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Points $A(2, 1)$ and $B(8, 1)$ are endpoints of a segment. Point $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of $AB$ and has a $y$-coordinate of $5$. What is $PA$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$4$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-distance-to-the-midpoint"},{"text":"$3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-half-the-segment"},{"text":"$25$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-the-square-root"},{"text":"$5$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$5$$q$, $q$The midpoint of $AB$ is $(5, 1)$ and $AB$ is horizontal, so the perpendicular bisector is $x = 5$ and $P = (5, 5)$. Then $PA = \sqrt{(5 - 2)^2 + (5 - 1)^2} = \sqrt{9 + 16} = 5$, and $PB$ is also $5$. The value $4$ is only the distance from $P$ down to the midpoint.$q$, 'draft', null)
